title: Midnight Blizzard / Microsoft
page: breaches/midnight-blizzard-microsoft.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: Midnight Blizzard password-sprayed a forgotten test tenant and crossed a legacy OAuth app into executive email; per Microsoft's March update, information from that email was used to gain, or attempt to gain, access, including to some source code.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 1 | account with no MFA, in a test tenant, was the way in | its password was weak and guessable
stat: ~7 | weeks from first access to detection | spray from late November 2023; detected January 12, 2024
stat: 10x | rise in password spray attempts in February | as much as ten times January's already-large volume, per Microsoft
Email became the next credential store: the actor tried to use secrets found in it, Microsoft said in March.
notes:
Start with the page's headline stats.

The page names the actor as Midnight Blizzard, the Russian state actor also known as APT29 and NOBELIUM. From late November 2023 it ran a password spray against a legacy, non-production test tenant belonging to Microsoft. One account there had a guessable password and no multi-factor authentication, or MFA. MFA asks for a second proof of identity beyond the password.

Microsoft detected the attack on January 12, 2024. The page puts that at about seven weeks from first access to detection, and gives the span as approximate, so keep the word about.

The third tile needs its qualifier. Microsoft reported password spray attempts running as much as tenfold higher in February than in the already-large January volume. Say as much as when you give the figure, and say it came from Microsoft.

The line under the tiles is the page's third headline stat. In March, Microsoft said the actor was attempting to use secrets it had found in that email, some of them shared between customers and Microsoft. The page calls that update the part that matters most.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: From late November 2023 | Password spray against a legacy, non-production Microsoft test tenant
event: January 12, 2024 | Microsoft detects the attack
event: January 19 | Microsoft discloses it, a week later
event: February | Password spray attempts up as much as 10x over January, per Microsoft
event: March 2024 | Microsoft's update: information from the email used to gain, or attempt to gain, access
That access included some source code repositories and internal systems, Microsoft said. The page gives no date for the mailbox access or for the use of what was taken. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The timeline shows only the dates the page gives.

From late November 2023, Midnight Blizzard ran a password spray against a legacy, non-production test tenant belonging to Microsoft. Microsoft detected the attack on January 12, 2024, and disclosed it a week later, on January 19.

Microsoft later reported that password spray attempts had risen as much as tenfold in February over the January volume. The page reports that figure as part of the March update.

In March 2024, Microsoft's update said the actor was using information from that email to gain, or attempt to gain, further access, which had included access to some source code repositories and internal systems. It also said the actor was attempting to use secrets it had found, some of them shared between customers and Microsoft.

Two things are not on the timeline because the page does not date them: when the actor accessed the mailboxes, and when it used what it took from them. Keep both undated when you present.

The markers are evenly spaced, so do not read the gaps between them as durations.

== When Microsoft disclosed, and what March added
layout: compare
col: January 2024 | Microsoft detected the attack on January 12 | It disclosed it a week later, on January 19
col: Microsoft's March 2024 update | Information from the email used to gain, or attempt to gain, access | That included access to some source code repositories and internal systems | The actor tried to use secrets it found, some shared with customers
The page calls the March update the part that matters most, and says the revision deserves credit for being published.
notes:
The page cites Microsoft's January disclosure and its March update, and treats the March update as the part that matters most.

In January 2024, Microsoft detected the attack on January 12 and disclosed it a week later, on January 19.

The March update is what the page calls the revision. Microsoft said the actor was using information from that email to gain, or attempt to gain, further access, which had included access to some source code repositories and internal systems. It also said the actor was attempting to use secrets it had found, some of them shared between customers and Microsoft. The same update reported password spray attempts as much as tenfold higher in February than in January; that figure is on the timeline.

Keep the word some, as the page does.

The page calls the March update the part that matters most, and the most valuable part of this chain. It also says the revision deserves credit for being published. That is the page's own judgment, so present it as the page's.

The page does not say what the January disclosure contained beyond its date, so this deck leaves it there.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | Password spray on a forgotten test tenant | T1110.003
step: 02 | Privilege Escalation | A legacy OAuth app with production rights | T1550.001
step: 03 | Collection | Executive, security and legal mail | T1114.002
step: 04 | Credential Access | Secrets in that email | T1552.001
Arrows show the page's stage order.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's four stages, each with its phase and primary ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01 is initial access: a low-and-slow password spray that found an account with a weak password and no MFA in a legacy, non-production test tenant.

Stage 02 is privilege escalation. The compromised test account could reach a legacy OAuth application holding elevated permissions into Microsoft's corporate environment. The page calls this the hinge.

Stage 03 is collection: the actor accessed a very small percentage of Microsoft's corporate mailboxes, including those of senior leadership and the cybersecurity, legal and other functions, and exfiltrated some emails and attachments.

Stage 04 is credential access. Microsoft's March update disclosed that the actor was using information from that email to gain, or attempt to gain, access, including to some source code repositories and internal systems, and was attempting to use secrets it found.

The next slides take each stage in turn.

== A password spray found an account with no MFA
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1110.003 | Brute Force: Password Spraying
technique: T1090.002 | Proxy: External Proxy
fact: Technique | tailored to a limited number of accounts, with few attempts
fact: Infrastructure | residential proxies, so attempts look like ordinary users
fact: The account | a guessable password, in a legacy, non-production test tenant
fact: Why it survived | not production, so not in the inventory that drives baselines
What made it succeed was the target, not the technique: a tenant outside whatever MFA enforcement and password policy governed Microsoft's real estate.
notes:
Stage 01 is initial access. The page maps it to T1110.003, Password Spraying, and T1090.002, External Proxy.

Password spraying tries a small number of common passwords against many accounts, staying under lockout thresholds and per-account rate limits. Run from a residential proxy network, it also defeats naive IP-based detection, because each attempt appears to come from a different ordinary address. Here, the page says, the spray was tailored to a limited number of accounts, with few attempts, to evade detection and avoid lockouts.

The page says what made it succeed was not the technique but the target. The test tenant was created for some purpose long since served, and sat outside whatever MFA enforcement and password policy governed Microsoft's real estate. Keep the word whatever: the page does not say what those controls were.

Nobody was reviewing the tenant because it was not production, and it did not appear in the inventories that drive security baselines. The page says that is precisely why it still existed.

The page sums it up: the initial access was an account nobody owned, in an environment nobody considered production.

The next slide explains, in general terms, what a tenant is and what MFA adds.

== What a tenant is, and what MFA adds
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, Tenant / Multi-Tenant | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-tenant
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary entries MFA and Phishing-Resistant MFA
A tenant is a logical boundary for one customer's data, identities and configuration in a shared platform.

MFA pairs something you know with something you have, or are. Phishing-resistant MFA cannot be relayed or replayed.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains two general ideas from the csoh.org glossary.

The glossary defines a tenant as a logical boundary for one customer's data, identities and configuration in a shared platform. Multi-tenant software as a service keeps many tenants isolated on the same infrastructure.

It defines MFA, multi-factor authentication, as something you know plus something you have, or something you are.

It defines phishing-resistant MFA as multi-factor methods that cannot be relayed or replayed by an attacker, because the second factor is cryptographically bound to the origin. Its examples are FIDO2 security keys, passkeys and smartcards. It says push prompts, one-time password codes and SMS are explicitly not phishing-resistant.

Phishing-resistant MFA is one of the page's defenses, and it comes back after the stages.

Also from the csoh.org glossary entry MFA: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-mfa

And from the entry Phishing-Resistant MFA: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-phishing-resistant

== A legacy OAuth app held production rights
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Privilege Escalation
technique: T1550.001 | Application Access Token
technique: T1098.002 | Additional Email Delegate Permissions
fact: The bridge | legacy OAuth app with elevated corporate-environment rights
fact: Actor actions | granted itself access; created additional malicious OAuth apps
fact: Reached | Office 365 Exchange Online mailboxes
fact: Two failures | non-prod identity reaching prod; an old grant nobody revisited
The page calls this the hinge, the reason a test-environment compromise became a corporate one.
notes:
Stage 02 is privilege escalation. The page maps it to T1550.001, Application Access Token, and T1098.002, Additional Email Delegate Permissions.

OAuth is an authorization standard that lets an application reach a service with a token in place of a user's password.

The compromised test account could reach a legacy OAuth application that had been granted elevated permissions into Microsoft's corporate environment. The application was registered long ago. The page says that configuration presumably made sense when it was created and had simply never been revisited. Keep the word presumably.

The actor used that application to grant itself access, creating additional malicious OAuth applications along the way and using them to reach Office 365 Exchange Online mailboxes. Exchange Online is Microsoft's hosted email service. The page's defenses add Microsoft's account from its responder guidance: the actor created new OAuth applications, created a new user account to consent to them, and used the legacy app to grant them the Office 365 Exchange Online full_access_as_app role, an application role that allows access to mailboxes.

The page names two failures. A non-production identity should not be able to reach a production application, and an old application should not still hold rights nobody would grant today.

The next slide draws the path the page describes.

== From a test tenant to corporate mailboxes, per the page
layout: layers
layer: Non-production | Legacy test tenant: one account, weak password, no MFA
layer: The bridge | Legacy OAuth app with elevated corporate-environment rights
layer: Actor-created | Additional malicious OAuth apps, created along the way
layer: Reached | Office 365 Exchange Online mailboxes
Each arrow is a step the page states. The page does not say where the legacy app was registered.
notes:
This slide draws the path stage 02 describes. Each arrow is a step the page states.

The compromised account sat in a legacy, non-production test tenant, with a weak password and no MFA. That account could reach a legacy OAuth application that held elevated permissions into Microsoft's corporate environment. The actor used that application to grant itself access, creating additional malicious OAuth applications along the way, and used them to reach Office 365 Exchange Online mailboxes.

The bridge sits in its own row because the page does not say which tenant the legacy application was registered in, or how its permissions were granted. Do not fill that in when you present.

The page's point is the crossing itself: a non-production identity reached a production application. It says "it's only test" describes the governance, never the reachability.

== What an app registration, a service principal and consent are
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, application and service principal objects | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/app-objects-and-service-principals
credit: Also from Overview of permissions and consent (learn.microsoft.com)
An application object defines an app in its home tenant. A service principal represents the app in each tenant using it, and sets what it can do there.

Consent is how users or admins authorize an app's access.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains three ideas in general, from Microsoft Learn, and says nothing about the incident.

Registering an application with a Microsoft Entra tenant creates its one application object, in the tenant where it was registered, called its home tenant. The application object is a template from which service principals are created. A service principal is created in every tenant where the application is used, and it defines what the app can do there, who can access it and what resources it can reach. The Microsoft Entra admin center lists application objects on its App registrations page and service principals on its Enterprise applications page.

Consent is a process where users or admins authorize an application to access a protected resource. Delegated permissions, also called scopes, let an app act for a signed-in user. Application permissions let it act with no user signed in.

The page does not say what kind of permissions the legacy application itself held.

Also from Overview of permissions and consent in the Microsoft identity platform: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/permissions-consent-overview

== The actor accessed a very small percentage of Microsoft's corporate mailboxes
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Collection
technique: T1114.002 | Email Collection: Remote Email Collection
fact: Included | senior leadership, cybersecurity, legal and other functions
fact: Taken | some emails and attached documents
fact: Initial interest | information about Midnight Blizzard itself, Microsoft stated
The page says a security team's mail can show an intruder an investigation forming, and calls that the strongest possible argument for out-of-band incident response.
notes:
Stage 03 is collection. The page maps it to T1114.002, Remote Email Collection.

The actor accessed a very small percentage of Microsoft's corporate email accounts, including those of senior leadership and employees in the cybersecurity, legal and other functions, and exfiltrated some emails and attached documents. Microsoft stated the actor was initially targeting email accounts for information related to Midnight Blizzard itself.

The page reads the mailbox selection as intelligence work rather than opportunism: senior leadership for strategy, legal for what is contested and disclosed, and cybersecurity for what the defender knows. Present that as the page's reading.

Mail from the cybersecurity function was among what the actor accessed. The page says an actor who can read a security team's mail during an intrusion has an advantage that is difficult to overstate, because that mail can show an investigation forming. It calls that the strongest possible argument for keeping incident response communications on an out-of-band channel, one that does not depend on the environment being investigated. That is a general point; the page does not say what this actor saw.

Microsoft detected the attack on January 12, 2024, and disclosed it on January 19.

The next slide explains, in general terms, what ATT&CK means by remote email collection.

== What ATT&CK means by remote email collection
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1114.002 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1114/002/
ATT&CK says adversaries may target an Exchange server, Office 365 or Google Workspace to collect sensitive information. They may reach externally facing email services with credentials or access tokens.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

Remote Email Collection is a sub-technique of Email Collection. ATT&CK says adversaries may target an Exchange server, Office 365 or Google Workspace to collect sensitive information.

It describes two routes. Adversaries may use a user's credentials and interact directly with the Exchange server to acquire information from within a network. They may also access externally facing Exchange services, Office 365 or Google Workspace to access email using credentials or access tokens.

== Per Microsoft, the actor tried to use secrets
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1552.001 | Unsecured Credentials: Credentials In Files
fact: Information | from the email, used to gain, or attempt to gain, access
fact: Included | access to some source code repositories and internal systems
fact: Secrets | some exchanged with customers, whom Microsoft contacted
fact: Microsoft says | no evidence Microsoft-hosted customer-facing systems were compromised
The page's transferable lesson: every mailbox is a credential store.
notes:
Stage 04 is credential access. The page maps it to T1552.001, Credentials In Files.

Microsoft's March update said two things, and the page keeps them apart. The actor was using information from the exfiltrated email to gain, or attempt to gain, unauthorized access, and that had included access to some source code repositories and internal systems. Separately, it was attempting to use secrets of different types it had found, some shared between customers and Microsoft, and Microsoft was reaching out to those customers. The page does not say the customer-shared secrets reached the source code, so keep the two apart.

People send secrets in email, the page says: connection strings while debugging, API keys to a partner, a password just this once in a thread that never gets deleted. A connection string tells an application how to reach a database, and can include credentials.

Microsoft also reported password spray attempts as much as tenfold higher in February than in the already-large January volume. Its position, as of March 2024, is that it had found no evidence that Microsoft-hosted customer-facing systems were compromised. Give that as Microsoft's statement, with its date.

The page's lesson is to assume every mailbox in your organization contains credentials.

== An OAuth grant that outlived the reason it was made
layout: text
The page sees one shape in Vercel / Context.ai, Salesloft Drift and UNC6040: an OAuth grant outliving its reason.
- Non-production identities should not reach production applications.
- Old applications should not hold rights nobody would grant today.
notes:
The page draws a pattern from stage 02.

It says the general shape recurs across this site: the Vercel / Context.ai, Salesloft Drift and UNC6040 kill chains all turn on an OAuth grant outliving the reason it was made. Each has its own kill chain on csoh.org, linked at the end of this deck.

Here, the page names two failures. A non-production identity should not be able to reach a production application, and an old application should not still hold rights nobody would grant today. The legacy application's configuration presumably made sense when it was created and had simply never been revisited.

The page's first defense makes the same point: "it's only test" describes the governance, never the reachability.

== Six defenses against four stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04
row: Non-prod tenants on the production baseline | 01
row: OAuth app permissions audited and expired | 02
row: Phishing-resistant MFA, no environment exceptions | 01
row: Password spray detected on the pattern | 01
row: Mailboxes hunted as credential stores | 04
row: Incident response communications out of band | 03
Each stage has at least one defense the page ties to it.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against its four stages. A cell is marked only where the defense's own words name something from that stage.

Three defenses answer stage 01. Bringing non-production tenants under the production baseline names the environment where the chain started, and MFA and password policy. Phishing-resistant MFA everywhere answers the password spray, which the page says only works against accounts without a second factor. Detecting spray on the pattern names residential proxies and non-production accounts.

Auditing OAuth application permissions answers stage 02: the page says a legacy app with production rights turned a test-tenant compromise into a corporate one.

Keeping incident response communications out of band answers stage 03: the page says the actor reached mailboxes in Microsoft's cybersecurity function.

Treating mailboxes as credential stores answers stage 04, where secrets exchanged with customers were in the mailboxes.

The mailbox defense also names the mailboxes stage 03 collected, but it acts on the secrets inside them, so it is marked at stage 04 only. Likewise, the baseline defense's line about reachability touches the crossing at stage 02, but it acts on tenants, so it is marked at stage 01 only.

== Bring non-production tenants under the baseline, and require phishing-resistant MFA
layout: compare
col: One baseline, or delete them (stage 01) | Inventory every tenant, subscription and directory you own | Include the ones created for a demo three years ago | Enforce MFA and password policy uniformly
col: Phishing-resistant MFA (stage 01) | Everywhere, with no environment-based exceptions | Password spray only works against accounts without a second factor, the page says
"It's only test" describes the governance, never the reachability.
notes:
Two defenses answer the way in.

First, bring non-production tenants under the same identity baseline as production, or delete them. The page says the entire chain started in an environment excluded from the controls that protected everything else. Inventory every tenant, subscription and directory your organization owns, including the ones created for a demo three years ago, and enforce MFA and password policy uniformly.

Second, enforce phishing-resistant MFA everywhere, with no environment-based exceptions. The page says password spray only works against accounts without a second factor. It calls this the same conclusion as the 0ktapus kill chain, arriving by a completely different route, and says the exception list is where both attacks look first.

The line under the columns is the page's: "it's only test" describes the governance, never the reachability.

== Detect password spray on the pattern, not the volume from one address
layout: compare
col: Alert on (stage 01) | A single password tried across many accounts | Attempts against dormant or non-production accounts | Successful logins from networks (ASNs) your workforce never uses
col: Disable (stage 01) | Legacy authentication protocols that bypass modern controls
Residential proxies defeat per-IP thresholds, the page says.
notes:
This defense also answers stage 01.

Residential proxies defeat per-IP thresholds, because each attempt appears to come from a different ordinary address. So the page says to alert on the pattern instead.

Alert on a single password tried across many accounts. Alert on authentication attempts against dormant or non-production accounts. And alert on successful logins from ASNs your workforce never uses. An ASN, an autonomous system number, identifies a network on the internet, such as an internet provider's.

The page also says legacy authentication protocols that bypass modern controls should be disabled outright. It does not say whether the actor used them here.

== Audit OAuth app permissions, and expire the ones nobody can justify
layout: compare
col: Review (stage 02) | Enumerate application registrations and service principals | Review granted scopes against current need | Remove standing high-privilege consent
col: Alert (stage 02) | On new credentials added to existing service principals | On any grant of a mailbox-wide role such as full_access_as_app
A legacy app with production rights turned a test-tenant compromise into a corporate one.
notes:
This defense answers stage 02.

The page says a legacy app with production rights turned a test-tenant compromise into a corporate one.

Enumerate application registrations and service principals. Review their granted scopes against current need, and remove standing high-privilege consent.

Then alert on new credentials being added to existing service principals, and on any grant of a mailbox-wide role such as full_access_as_app. Microsoft's responder guidance, as the page gives it, says the actor here created new OAuth applications, created a new user account to consent to them, and used the legacy app to grant them the Office 365 Exchange Online full_access_as_app role.

The page frames this defense around old grants: expire the permissions nobody can justify.

== Hunt mailboxes for secrets; keep incident response out of band
layout: compare
col: Mailboxes (stage 04) | Run secret scanning across mail, not just repositories | Share credentials through a channel other than email | Rotate anything sent by mail
col: Incident response (stage 03) | Pre-established: separate tenant, separate provider, or offline | Use it by default while a compromise's scope is still unknown
The actor reached mailboxes in Microsoft's cybersecurity function, the page says.
notes:
Two defenses answer the last two stages.

For stage 04, treat mailboxes as credential stores and hunt them accordingly. Run secret scanning across mail, not just repositories. Establish a channel for sharing credentials with partners and customers so that email is never the mechanism, and rotate anything that has been sent by mail. The page notes that Microsoft had to contact customers because secrets exchanged with them were in those mailboxes.

For stage 03, keep incident response communications out of band. The page says the actor reached mailboxes in Microsoft's cybersecurity function and, per Microsoft, was initially looking for information about Midnight Blizzard itself. Have a pre-established channel for incident response coordination: a separate tenant, a separate provider, or offline. Use it by default when the scope of a compromise is still unknown.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Can you list every tenant, subscription and directory you own, including old demo ones?
check: Do non-production tenants get the same MFA and password policy as production?
check: Is your MFA phishing-resistant, with no environment-based exceptions?
check: Which app registrations and service principals hold standing high-privilege consent?
check: Would new service principal credentials, or a full_access_as_app grant, raise an alert?
check: Do you alert on one password tried across many accounts, not just per IP?
check: Do you scan mail for secrets, and is an out-of-band incident response channel ready?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether you could list every tenant, subscription and directory your organization owns, including the ones created for a demo years ago, and whether they all get the same MFA and password policy as production. Ask whether your MFA is phishing-resistant everywhere, with no environment-based exceptions.

Ask which application registrations and service principals hold standing high-privilege consent, and whether new credentials added to an existing service principal, or a grant of a mailbox-wide role such as full_access_as_app, would raise an alert.

Ask whether you detect a single password tried across many accounts, attempts against dormant or non-production accounts, and logins from ASNs your workforce never uses, and whether legacy authentication protocols are disabled.

Finally, ask whether anyone runs secret scanning across mail, whether anything sent by mail gets rotated, and whether a pre-established, out-of-band channel for incident response is ready to use.

== Which tenant, app or grant in your estate does nobody own?
layout: question
The way in was an account nobody owned, in an environment nobody considered production. The bridge was an old grant nobody revisited.

Name one in your own estate. How would you find it?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The page sums up stage 01 as an account nobody owned, in an environment nobody considered production. At stage 02, a legacy OAuth application still held elevated permissions into the corporate environment, a configuration the page says had simply never been revisited.

Ask each person to name one specific example from their own estate: a test tenant, a demo subscription, an old application registration. Then ask how they would find the ones they cannot name. The answer slide gives what the page says.

== What the page says about forgotten tenants and grants
layout: answer
"It's only test" describes the governance, never the reachability.
- Inventory every tenant, subscription and directory; enforce MFA and password policy uniformly.
- Review OAuth grants against current need, and remove standing high-privilege consent.
notes:
The page's answer has two parts.

For tenants, inventory every tenant, subscription and directory your organization owns, including the ones created for a demo three years ago, and enforce MFA and password policy uniformly. Bring non-production tenants under the same identity baseline as production, or delete them.

For grants, enumerate application registrations and service principals, review their granted scopes against current need, remove standing high-privilege consent, and alert on new credentials added to existing service principals and on any grant of a mailbox-wide role such as full_access_as_app.

Bring the group's examples back to the page's line: "it's only test" describes the governance, never the reachability.

== What would an attacker find in your mailboxes?
layout: question
At stage 04, Microsoft said the actor was attempting to use secrets it found in the email, some shared between customers and Microsoft.

Think of your own organization's mail. Which credentials would a search turn up?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one too.

At stage 04, Microsoft's March update said the actor was attempting to use secrets of different types it had found in the exfiltrated email, some shared between customers and Microsoft, and that Microsoft was reaching out to those customers. The page lists the kinds of secret people send by email: connection strings while debugging, API keys to a partner, a password just this once in a thread that never gets deleted.

Ask each person to picture a search of their own organization's mail. What would it find, and would anyone know it had been run? The answer slide gives what the page says.

== What the page says about secrets in email
layout: answer
The page says to assume every mailbox in your organization contains credentials.
- Run secret scanning across mail, not just repositories.
- Share credentials through a channel other than email, and rotate anything sent by mail.
notes:
The page's answer is to treat mailboxes as credential stores and hunt them accordingly.

Run secret scanning across mail, not just repositories. Establish a channel for sharing credentials with partners and customers so that email is never the mechanism. Rotate anything that has been sent by mail.

The page's reason is this incident: Microsoft had to contact customers because secrets exchanged with them were in those mailboxes.

Bring the group's answers back to this. If someone pictured a credential in their own mail, the page's guidance is that it should be rotated, and that the next one should travel by another channel.

== Sources
layout: links
link: MSRC, January disclosure | https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/msrc/blog/2024/01/microsoft-actions-following-attack-by-nation-state-actor-midnight-blizzard
link: MSRC, March update (source code and internal systems) | https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/msrc/blog/2024/03/update-on-microsoft-actions-following-attack-by-nation-state-actor-midnight-blizzard
